---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import PageIntro from '../../components/PageIntro.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import Cmd from '../../components/Cmd.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import RepeatMistakes from '../../components/RepeatMistakes.astro';
import { faqPick, site } from '../../content/site';

const title = 'CLAUDE.md vs MEMORY.md vs hippo: what goes where';
const description =
  "What belongs in CLAUDE.md, what Claude Code's auto memory keeps, and where hippo fits. In our own test on 29 repositories, against built-in memory alone, adding hippo took repeat mistakes from 21 in 100 to 11.";
const docs = 'https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory';

const cards = [
  { name: 'CLAUDE.md', body: 'Rules you write for every session, such as build commands and code style.' },
  { name: 'Auto memory', body: 'Leave this to Claude, which notes your corrections and preferences.' },
  {
    name: 'hippo',
    body: 'What comes up as you work, such as a failed command or a fact that changed. It is recalled when it matches the task and shared with your other agents.',
  },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="CLAUDE.md vs memory" published="2026-09-28">
  <Nav />
  <main id="main">
    <PageIntro>
      <Fragment slot="title">CLAUDE.md, auto memory and hippo: <span class="text-gradient">what goes where</span></Fragment>
      <p>
        Claude Code has two memories of its own, the <code>CLAUDE.md</code> files you write and the auto memory it writes. Hippo runs beside both. This page says what belongs in each, and what we measured when hippo was added.
      </p>
    </PageIntro>

    <Section title="Claude Code's two memories">
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>
          <code>CLAUDE.md</code> files hold instructions you write, at managed, user, project and local level. Every file found is added to context, and none overrides another.
        </li>
        <li>
          Auto memory holds notes Claude writes from your corrections and preferences, in a <code>MEMORY.md</code> index with one topic file per memory. It is on by default and stays on the machine. Each session loads the first 200 lines of <code>MEMORY.md</code> or the first 25KB, whichever comes first.
        </li>
        <li>
          Both are context, and neither is enforced. When two rules contradict each other, Claude may pick one arbitrarily (<a class="link" href={docs} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Claude Code's memory docs<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>, checked 12 November 2026).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Section>

    <Section title="What goes where" alt>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
        {cards.map((c) => (
          <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="text-base font-medium text-zinc-100">{c.name}</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed">{c.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </Section>

    <section id="measured-against-built-in-memory" class="relative border-t border-rule">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 py-(--section-y)">
        <h2 class="max-w-3xl text-balance text-3xl leading-tight sm:text-4xl">Measured against built-in memory</h2>
        <p class="mt-6 mb-8 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-zinc-400">
          Hippo runs beside Claude Code's own memory, so the fair test is built-in memory alone against built-in memory plus hippo on the same tasks. We ran it on 29 repositories, registered first.
        </p>
        <RepeatMistakes />
        <p class="mt-8 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-zinc-400">
          Built-in memory does most of the work, taking repeats from 83 in 100 to 21. Adding hippo took them to 11 at the same cost. We can't yet say why, because the gain was the same with lesson capture switched off. One early clue, from a debugging read and no verdict, is that in the capture-off arm Claude Code's own memory already held the taught rule at 112 of 162 first chances to repeat.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl">
          <a class="link" href={site.links.z0Round3} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The round 3 result<span class="sr-only"> (opens in new tab)</span></a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <Section title="Bringing CLAUDE.md notes into hippo" alt>
      <Cmd lines={['hippo import --claude CLAUDE.md --dry-run', 'hippo import --claude CLAUDE.md']} />
      <p>
        The import skips hippo's own block and any duplicates. After that, <code>hippo sleep</code> at the end of each session also imports new auto memory notes, so they reach the store your other agents read.
      </p>
    </Section>

    <Faq items={faqPick(['Do I still need CLAUDE.md?'])} heading="CLAUDE.md questions" moreHref="/faq/" />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
